// @8bitscript/core — the portable standard library.
//
// This file is the module entry point named by the "8bitscript".entry field in
// this package's package.json. It is what the compiler's resolver loads when a
// project writes:
//
//     import { screen, input } from "@8bitscript/core";
//
// INTENDED SURFACE, NOT AN IMPLEMENTATION. The bodies are deliberately absent:
// 8BitScript's declaration syntax — how a module exports a namespace like
// `screen`, how extern and target-conditional declarations are written — is not
// designed yet, and guessing at it here would bake a guess into the first
// source file in the repository. The entries below record what the portable
// surface is meant to cover; they become real declarations once the module
// system is specified.
//
//   screen.putChar(x: u8, y: u8, code: u8): void
//       Write a character code to a screen cell.
//
//   input.left(): bool
//   input.right(): bool
//   input.up(): bool
//   input.down(): bool
//   input.fire(): bool
//       Read the current state of the standard directional/fire controls.
//
// Everything here must behave identically on every target. Anything that
// cannot — hardware registers, target-specific memory maps — belongs in a
// target package such as @8bitscript/vic20 instead.
